# TableGrid Component

Component: `frontend-nx/apps/edu-hub/components/common/TableGrid/index.tsx`
Types: `frontend-nx/apps/edu-hub/components/common/TableGrid/types.ts`

## Basic Usage

```typescript
import TableGrid from '../common/TableGrid';
import { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/react-table';

const columns = useMemo<ColumnDef<MyRow>[]>(() => [
  { header: 'ID', accessorKey: 'id', size: 80 },
  { header: 'Title', accessorKey: 'title', size: 300, minSize: 200 },
], []);

<TableGrid<MyRow>
  columns={columns}
  data={data}
  loading={loading}
  error={error}
  totalCount={totalCount}
  pageIndex={pageIndex}
  onPageChange={setPageIndex}
  pageSize={pageSize}
  onPageSizeChange={handlePageSizeChange}
  searchFilter={searchFilter}
  onSearchFilterChange={setSearchFilter}
/>
```

## Column Sizing Guidelines

| Content Type | Size | Example |
|--------------|------|---------|
| Status/Icon | 50-100px | `size: 80` |
| Numeric/ID | 80-150px | `size: 100` |
| Content | 200-500px | `size: 300, minSize: 200` |
| Actions | 100-200px | `size: 150` |

## useTableGrid Hook

```typescript
import { useTableGrid } from '../common/TableGrid/hooks';

const { data, loading, error, searchFilter, pageIndex, setSearchFilter, setPageIndex } = useTableGrid({
  queryHook: useAdminQuery,
  query: MY_QUERY,
  queryVariables: { programId },
  pageSize: 20,
  refetchFilter: useCallback((searchTerm) => ({
    where: { title: { _ilike: `%${searchTerm}%` } }
  }), []),
});
```

## Multi-Word Search

```typescript
import { createMultiWordSearchCondition } from '../common/TableGrid/utils';

refetchFilter: (searchFilter) => ({
  where: createMultiWordSearchCondition(searchFilter, ['firstName', 'lastName', 'email'])
})
```

## Toolbar Filters

Multi-select facet filters render next to the search field. The caller owns the selection and
turns it into query variables, so the same control serves client- and server-side filtering.
Several selected values are meant as "any of them".

```typescript
filters={[{
  id: 'privileges',
  label: t('privileges_filter_label'),
  options: [{ value: 'events', label: t('can_manage_events') }],
  selected: privilegeFilter,
  onChange: (selected) => {
    setPrivilegeFilter(selected as AdminPrivilege[]);
    setPageIndex(0); // a changed filter changes what matches
  },
}]}
```

## Row Deletion

Both flavors render the same trash icon, confirmation dialog and error dialog; pick by whether
deleting a row is one mutation:

```typescript
// One mutation taking the row id
deleteMutation={DELETE_THING}
deleteIdType="number"          // or "uuidString"
deleteVariableName="id"        // renames the mutation variable
validateDeleteResult={...}     // surfaces a Hasura action's own failure

// Or a caller-owned deletion, for rows that take more than one mutation
onRowDelete={async (row) => { ... }}  // rejection opens the error dialog; refetching is yours
```

`canDeleteRow` disables the control per row, and `generateDeletionConfirmationQuestion` makes the
confirmation name what is about to be deleted. Omit both props to leave the column out entirely —
which is how a delete control is limited to a role.

## Key Rules

- ✅ Always memoize columns with `useMemo`
- ✅ Use `size` property (not legacy `meta.width`)
- ✅ Reset pageIndex when filters/pageSize change
- ✅ Use `filters` for facet filtering instead of building a toolbar control
- ✅ Name the deleted item in `generateDeletionConfirmationQuestion`
- ✅ Synchronize pageSize between hook and component
- ❌ Don't manage offset manually - let useTableGrid handle it
